WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.600
A useful GPU cost model looks beyond the visible price of a compute instance.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:17.600
The workload may consume GPU capacity, attached CPU and memory, local disks, remote volumes, object storage, snapshots, network transfer, managed orchestration, logging, monitoring, and support time from engineers and operators.

3
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:21.200
Price and effective cost are not the same thing.

4
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:32.400
A lower-priced resource that sits idle, waits on storage, fails repeatedly, or requires manual recovery may be more expensive than a better-matched resource that completes reliably.

5
00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:38.000
Effective cost asks how much useful output the team receives for the money spent.

6
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.400
Capacity choices also have tradeoffs.

7
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:43.600
On-demand usage can be simple and flexible.

8
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:50.000
Reserved or committed capacity can help predictable demand but may create waste if the workload changes.

9
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:58.400
Interruptible or preemptible capacity can reduce spend when jobs can tolerate stops and restarts, but it needs checkpointing and retry design.

10
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:01.200
Hidden costs often live around the job.

11
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.600
Large datasets may be copied repeatedly.

12
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:06.000
Checkpoints may accumulate.

13
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.200
Logs and metrics may grow without retention rules.

14
00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:14.000
Artifacts from old experiments may remain long after the decision they supported.

15
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.200
Operational labor should be part of the picture.

16
00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:24.000
If a workload requires frequent manual cleanup, investigation, reruns, or emergency budget review, that effort has cost.

17
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:31.200
A good model explains both what the platform charges and how well the workload uses what it allocates.

18
00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:38.000
It also helps teams compare alternatives without pretending that the listed compute rate is the whole story.
